      "text": "To solve the problem, we need to determine the area of the square and the areas of the four circles, and then find the area of the shaded region by subtracting the area of the four circles from the area of the square.\n\nFirst, let's find the side length of the square. The square has a side length of 20 inches.\n\nNext, we calculate the area of the square:\n\\[\n\\text{Area of the square} = \\text{side length}^2 = 20^2 = 400 \\text{ square inches}\n\\]\n\nNow, we need to find the area of one circle. Since the circles are tangent to two sides of the square and their adjacent circles, the diameter of each circle is equal to the side length of the square divided by 2. Therefore, the diameter of each circle is:\n\\[\n\\text{Diameter of each circle} = \\frac{20}{2} = 10 \\text{ inches}\n\\]\nThe radius of each circle is half of the diameter:\n\\[\n\\text{Radius of each circle} = \\frac{10}{2} = 5 \\text{ inches}\n\\]\nThe area of one circle is:\n\\[\n\\text{Area of one circle} = \\pi \\times \\text{radius}^2 = \\pi \\times 5^2 = 25\\pi \\text{ square inches}\n\\]\nSince there are four circles, the total area of the four circles is:\n\\[\n\\text{Total area of the four circles} = 4 \\times 25\\pi = 100\\pi \\text{ square inches}\n\\]\nThe area of the shaded region is the area of the square minus the total area of the four circles:\n\\[\n\\text{Area of the shaded region} = \\text{Area of the square} - \\text{Total area of the four circles} = 400 - 100\\pi\n\\]\nTherefore, the area of the shaded region is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{400 - 100\\pi}\n\\]",
