      "text": "To solve this problem, we need to determine the area of the shaded regions in Wei's logo. The logo consists of several circles and a square, with each circle being tangent to two sides of the square and its two adjacent circles. Here's a step-by-step solution:\n\n1. **Determine the radius of each circle:**\n   Each circle is inscribed in a sector of the square, and the diagonal of the square is equal to \\(4\\sqrt{2}\\) inches (since the diagonal of a square with side length \\(s\\) is \\(s\\sqrt{2}\\)). The radius of each circle is the perpendicular distance from the center of the circle to the adjacent sides of the square, which is \\(1\\) inch.\n\n2. **Calculate the area of one circle:**\n   The area of one circle is given by the formula \\(\\pi r^2\\), where \\(r\\) is the radius. Here, \\(r = 1\\) inch.\n   \\[\n   \\text{Area of one circle} = \\pi \\times 1^2 = \\pi \\text{ square inches}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Calculate the total area of all four circles:**\n   Since there are four circles, the total area of the circles is:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Total area of circles} = 4 \\times \\pi = 4\\pi \\text{ square inches}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Determine the area of the square:**\n   The area of the square is:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Area of the square} = 4 \\times 4 = 16 \\text{ square inches}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Calculate the shaded area:**\n   The shaded area is the area of the square minus the area of the four circles:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Shaded area} = 16 - 4\\pi \\text{ square inches}\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the number of square inches that will be shaded is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{16 - 4\\pi}\n\\]",
